Z||000003||Formula88||08-06-2001||01:44 PM||||I too, would like some advice on this repair.  Mine's a bit more damaged.  It appears eroded or worn just between the moulding and the tail lights.  It's got a grainy texture to the gray surface, like something has eaten away at the moulding.  Any suggestions?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Bad_bumper.jpg"> <BR>   <P>Man, this is weird.  PIP works perfect on my home PC, but I can't get it working on my laptop - even using the automatic configuration!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  I'll fix it from home so you can see what I mean.<P>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 08-06-2001).]<P>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 08-06-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 08-06-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||08-06-2001||04:17 PM||||Formula 88:<P>Try sanding it till smooth and see what you have. If the damage isn't deep reprime and paint.<P>JohnF:<P>There is a rubber bumper repair kit that can be purchased at a GOOD parts store. Follow the directions to fill your spot, sand, prime, paint and you should be OK.||||reg||1
